  "issue_number": 15589,
  "issue_title": "--mcp and --lsp are silently ignored when RuboCop server mode is running",
  "issue_body": "### Before submitting\n\n- [x] I have searched the existing issues and this hasn't been reported yet.\n- [x] I am running the latest stable version of RuboCop.\n\n### Issue type\n\nOther \u2014 a CLI/server-mode routing bug rather than a cop bug, so **Cop name** and **RuboCop configuration** are left blank. (Filed through the API rather than the issue form, hence the manual field headings.) The nearest existing issues I found are #11857, which introduced the `exec.rb` stdin behavior referenced below, and #14911, which added the MCP server; neither covers this.\n\n### Steps to reproduce the problem\n\nCheck `--server-status` immediately before each run \u2014 a stale PID file can report a server that has already exited, and the bug then appears not to reproduce.\n\n```console\n$ REQ='{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"capabilities\":{},\"clientInfo\":{\"name\":\"probe\",\"version\":\"1.0\"}}}'\n\n$ bundle exec rubocop --server-status\nRuboCop server is not running.\n$ { printf '%s\\n' \"$REQ\"; sleep 5; } | bundle exec rubocop --mcp\n{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"result\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"capabilities\":{...},\"serverInfo\":{\"name\":\"rubocop_mcp_server\",\"version\":\"1.89.0\"}}}\n\n$ bundle exec rubocop --start-server\nRuboCop server starting on 127.0.0.1:58232.\n$ bundle exec rubocop --server-status\nRuboCop server (56074) is running.\n$ { printf '%s\\n' \"$REQ\"; sleep 5; } | bundle exec rubocop --mcp\n$ echo $?\n0\n```\n\n`--stop-server` restores it and `--start-server` breaks it again, deterministically. `--lsp` behaves identically given a `Content-Length`-framed request.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`rubocop --mcp` should start the Model Context Protocol (MCP) server, and `rubocop --lsp` the Language Server Protocol (LSP) server, regardless of whether a RuboCop server is running for the directory.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nBoth flags are silently ignored while a server is running for the working directory: nothing is read from stdin, nothing written to stdout or stderr, exit `0`. An MCP client sees only the stdio pipe close mid-handshake, with nothing indicating server mode is involved \u2014 for us it surfaced as a bare `CONNECTION_CLOSED`.\n\nUnder `--debug` the run is visibly *successful* while producing no protocol output at all:\n\n```console\n$ { printf '%s\\n' \"$REQ\"; sleep 5; } | bundle exec rubocop --mcp --debug\nFor /path/to/project: configuration from /path/to/project/.rubocop.yml\nDefault configuration from .../rubocop-1.89.0/config/default.yml\n...\nFinished in 0.57698 seconds\n```\n\nThe behavior is stateful: it works with no server running and breaks once one is started \u2014 via `--start-server`, `--server`, or a `--server` entry in `.rubocop` or `RUBOCOP_OPTS`, which is what people do to speed up editor integration. Servers are long-lived and per-directory, so once one exists the breakage persists for that checkout while others keep working.\n\n#### Cause\n\n`exe/rubocop` routes to the server before `RuboCop::CLI` is reached:\n\n```ruby\n# exe/rubocop:11-16\nif RuboCop::Server.running?\n  exit_status = RuboCop::Server::ClientCommand::Exec.new.run\nelse\n  require 'rubocop'\n  exit_status = RuboCop::CLI.new.run\nend\n```\n\n`--mcp` and `--lsp` are implemented only inside `RuboCop::CLI` \u2014 `handle_exiting_options` dispatches them through `run_command(name)` (`lib/rubocop/cli.rb:205-213`), resolving to `Command::MCP` / `::LSP`. A running server makes that unreachable.\n\nThey also can't simply be routed *through* the server, because that channel is one-shot by construction: the client calls `socket.close_write` before reading anything back (`client_command/base.rb:28-33`) and the server blocks on `@socket.read` until that EOF (`socket_reader.rb:25`), so a second frame is impossible even in principle \u2014 while MCP and LSP are long-lived bidirectional protocols.\n\nWhat actually happens is subtler than the flag being dropped. The server does run `RuboCop::CLI.new.run(['--color', '--mcp'])` internally (`server_command/exec.rb:21-22`), so `Command::MCP` starts. But `SocketReader` has already swapped the stdio globals (`socket_reader.rb:27-32`): `$stdout` is the client's socket, and `$stdin` is `StringIO.new(request.body)` \u2014 empty, because `Exec` reads stdin only for `-s`/`--stdin` (`exec.rb:20`). `StdioTransport#open` loops on `$stdin.gets`, gets `nil` on the first read, exits, and returns `0`. Hence a silent, successful, empty run.\n\nThe other five `EXITING_OPTIONS` (`version`, `verbose_version`, `show_cops`, `list_enabled_cops_for`, `show_docs_url`) are one-shot and survive the round-trip intact \u2014 I diffed each with and without a server. `--lsp` and `--mcp` are the only two needing a live bidirectional channel, and the only two that break.\n\n#### Suggested fix\n\nExclude these two from the server branch so they fall through to `RuboCop::CLI`:\n\n```ruby\nif RuboCop::Server.running? && (ARGV & %w[--lsp --mcp]).empty?\n```\n\nTwo caveats in case they're useful. `ARGV` isn't the only source \u2014 `Options#parse` also prepends `.rubocop` and `RUBOCOP_OPTS` arguments (`options.rb:29-32`), and `--mcp` set that way reaches `EXITING_OPTIONS` too; `Server::CLI` already consults `ArgumentsEnv` / `ArgumentsFile` for `--server` (`server/cli.rb:78-81`), so there's precedent for handling both. And a guard here still wouldn't stop `Server::CLI#run` from *starting* a server when `--server` comes from those sources, spawning one nothing will use.\n\nThis costs nothing in performance: `LSP::Server` and `MCP::Server` each hold their own long-lived `LSP::Runtime` for the life of the process, so the server adds no warm-boot benefit for either flag.\n\nErroring out instead would also beat the current silent success, and there's precedent for the message style in `EXCLUSIVE_OPTIONS` (`server/cli.rb:74-76`). But falling through seems friendlier: neither flag has reason to conflict with a warm server, and an editor or MCP client starting up shouldn't need to know about server mode. Happy to open a PR for whichever you prefer.\n\n### RuboCop version\n\n```console\n$ bundle exec rubocop -V\n1.89.0 (using Parser 3.3.12.0, Prism 1.9.0, rubocop-ast 1.50.0, analyzing as Ruby 4.0, running on ruby 4.0.6) +server [arm64-darwin25]\n  - rubocop-capybara 3.0.0\n  - rubocop-factory_bot 2.28.0\n  - rubocop-performance 1.27.0\n  - rubocop-rails 2.37.0\n  - rubocop-rake 0.7.1\n  - rubocop-rspec 3.10.2\n  - rubocop-rspec_rails 2.32.0\n```\n\nRouting in `exe/rubocop` is unchanged on `master` (commit `69753ad9b`).\n",
